<quiz>
  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>2.3.3.5 Invert functions</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p class="tb-exercise">Exercise</p>
<p>Find the inverse of each of the following functions. Give each answer in terms of \(x\).</p>
<ol class="tb-parts">
  <li><div>\(f(x)= {@aa@} \Rightarrow f^{-1}(x) = \) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</div></li>
  <li><div>\(f(x)= {@bb@} \Rightarrow f^{-1}(x) = \) [[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</div></li>
  <li><div>\(f(x)= {@cc@} \Rightarrow f^{-1}(x) = \) [[input:ans3]] [[validation:ans3]] [[feedback:prt3]]</div></li>
  <li><div>\(\displaystyle f(x)= {@dd@} \Rightarrow f^{-1}(x) = \) [[input:ans4]] [[validation:ans4]] [[feedback:prt4]]</div></li>
</ol>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>The <strong>inverse function</strong> \(f^{-1}\) undoes \(f\): if \(f\) sends an input \(a\) to an output \(b\), then \(f^{-1}\) sends \(b\) back to \(a\). It is not the reciprocal \(\dfrac{1}{f(x)}\). To find it, write \(y = f(x)\), make \(x\) the subject, and then rename the variable so that the inverse is written as a function of \(x\).</p>
<ol class="tb-parts">
  <li><p>Write \(y = {@aa@}\). Subtract \({@a1@}\) and then divide by \({@a2@}\): \(x = \dfrac{y-{@a1@}}{ {@a2@} }\). Hence \(f^{-1}(x) = \displaystyle {@Ansa@}\). The order matters: \(f\) multiplies by \({@a2@}\) and then adds \({@a1@}\), so the inverse first subtracts \({@a1@}\) and then divides by \({@a2@}\).</p></li>
  <li><p>Write \(y = {@bb@}\). Dividing by \({@b1@}\) gives \(x = {@ev(b1*y)@}\), so \(f^{-1}(x) = {@Ansb@}\). This function is its own inverse.</p></li>
  <li><p>Write \(y = {@cc@}\). Dividing by \({@kc@}\) gives \(x = \dfrac{y}{ {@kc@} }\), so \(f^{-1}(x) = \displaystyle {@Ansc@}\).</p></li>
  <li><p>Write \(y = \displaystyle {@dd@}\). Multiply both sides by \(x+{@d2@}\) and divide by \(y\): \(x + {@d2@} = \dfrac{ {@d1@} }{y}\), so \(x = \dfrac{ {@d1@} }{y} - {@d2@} = \dfrac{ {@d1@} - {@d2@}y}{y}\). Hence \(f^{-1}(x) = \displaystyle {@Ansd@}\).</p></li>
</ol>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
